WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.560
In an era marked by heated claims of
media bias, journalists need to ensure

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:14.519
that news coverage is independent and
impartial. Education, the field I know

00:00:14.519 --> 00:00:18.690
best, is one where it should be easy to
meet this bar. To see where things stand,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:24.150
two colleagues and I analyzed major media
news coverage of federal education in

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:30.330
2009 and 2017. In 2017, Republicans
controlled the federal government and

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:36.170
proposed major new reforms. Back in '09, the same was true of Democrats. In

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:40.559
2017, news stories on Republican
proposals featured a negative slant

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:45.870
nearly half the time. On the other hand,
in '09, coverage of Obama's proposals

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:50.399
tilted negative less than five percent
of the time. This kind of thing might fly

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:55.079
on the editorial pages, but unless one
thinks that Democrats have a monopoly on

00:00:55.079 --> 00:01:00.539
educational wisdom, such reporting marks
an abject professional failure. And if

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:04.439
journalists can't even report impartially on education, it's scary to

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.960
contemplate how they approach more
polarizing questions.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:14.240
Do you think major media coverage is biased towards one political party? Let us know in your

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.930
comments. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:24.240
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

